the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{3}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={e}^{{y}^{′}}-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{y}^{′}),\\,g({y}^{′})={e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=\\sqrt{1+({y}^{′})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311823 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2e69075-ef65-5a18-b911-601b515a9a84" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying d'Alembert Differential Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050185 :topic/id #uuid "34c91ba2-4c7e-5fc4-8f4a-760354b8b507" :topic/math-academy-id 6692 :topic/title "d'Alembert Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af0eb0e7-c2ad-543a-8a2d-3366487a137d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466375} {:answer/id #uuid "be0f5088-19a3-5966-b248-a6dbacdd21a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be0f5088-19a3-5966-b248-a6dbacdd21a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466376} :answer-field/id #uuid "70481d76-e2f6-5286-9ab8-4f745ef3c13b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467031} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42de17ab-e1fe-51aa-aa8f-0acb94eec672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466377} {:answer/id #uuid "28c34297-41b3-5f84-b30f-fdfb15a95db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28c34297-41b3-5f84-b30f-fdfb15a95db4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466378} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6dde0cf-cc8a-56f6-8185-eec09db75d2d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467032} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc47315f-f583-591c-b628-14579fe7e869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466379} {:answer/id #uuid "54f45743-ac64-545f-aff2-866a4d7f66a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc47315f-f583-591c-b628-14579fe7e869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466379} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8a1687-0c30-5e3f-8c64-74bc6e1cef95" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a1da43a-e002-579e-9946-31a6ae805b48" :question/math-academy-id "q-337245" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a d'Alembert differential equation?\n- **I.** $y=x({y}^{′})^{3}+({y}^{′})^{2}$\n- **II.** $y-x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}+\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}}=0$\n- **III.** ${y}^{′}={x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{y}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is a d'Alembert differential equation if it can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ny=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are differentiable functions of ${y}^{′}.$ \n\nNote that, in the special case where $f({y}^{′})={y}^{′},$ the equation reduces to a Clairaut differential equation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a d'Alembert differential equation of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\nf({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{3},\\,g({y}^{′})=({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a d'Alembert differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x\\sqrt{1+{y}^{′}}-\\frac{1}{1+{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwhich is of the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a d'Alembert differential equation. Solving for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich cannot be written in the form $y=xf({y}^{′})+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311881 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229138 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f5976004-053f-58ec-9d75-54d43a419397" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Differential Operators Using the General Form" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050537 :topic/id #uuid "51e12769-3848-5c84-bd86-1c35f7ad69b3" :topic/math-academy-id 6707 :topic/title "Linear Differential Operators"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829f70b2-bc9f-5ed3-a8a5-c5af2183b454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466381} {:answer/id #uuid "8239bed1-39e7-552f-9f82-bceb56ef2f3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "t