96584484-5512-509e-800f-6471db43c063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467152} {:answer/id #uuid "16516cf2-e93e-56d9-87ff-8121d22c639c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96584484-5512-509e-800f-6471db43c063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467152} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bcaad00-18f9-53ba-b327-8a50a26521d9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "661fb44f-ce59-5065-83c7-98cb9e445e73" :question/math-academy-id "q-339711" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7ddb28ee5184cc0cc6dc0b7253d3dd3ed24481c8433e5937532345adb4162e3.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a node.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f82ff6ec76ab3cf26d2da516b6bf5c5d9773824043a38a26c8db8a16150bfc86.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314968 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306 :topic/id #uuid "eb34afb7-9644-525e-b42b-cf82816ee5e0" :topic/math-academy-id 3195 :topic/title "Introduction to Fourier Series"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1540307-c137-5f77-92f5-4b092cd23cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450524} {:answer/id #uuid "b26a5ad7-d9b6-5daf-a907-747628a40117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450525}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b26a5ad7-d9b6-5daf-a907-747628a40117" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450525} :answer-field/id #uuid "0274d08b-9380-569d-84c8-dc963ab100ba" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b606b4d-df6b-5a2a-b981-2ba28720e49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450526} {:answer/id #uuid "a115e870-9bf2-5ed8-8927-d64629e7f05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a115e870-9bf2-5ed8-8927-d64629e7f05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450527} :answer-field/id #uuid "c13b1094-b5e3-52f6-b339-eea66dbe3c10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9d3e61-d295-59d5-bc4a-a40f1598476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450528} {:answer/id #uuid "9795c2b2-56bc-5ba0-9f51-2a7159cef7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450529}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d9d3e61-d295-59d5-bc4a-a40f1598476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450528} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece68944-84b8-504a-b057-17e241701eea" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "68a9f1f0-e085-5ed7-bf4c-69fbdf0774fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-340510" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,3)$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+6)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3)$\n- **III.** $f(x+1)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $3$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+3)=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,3).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+6)=f((x+3)+3)=f(x+3)=f(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true by definition of the periodic extension.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=2.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2+1)=f(3)=F(3)=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2)=F(2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306 :topic/id #uuid "eb34afb7-9644-525e-b42b-cf82816ee5e0" :topic/math-academy-id 3195 :topic/title "Introduction to Fourier Series"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fa4792b-5b15-527a-992e-0d96170ab1aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450530} {:answer/id #uuid "f9461d03-b412-574d-9841-bce08dceb69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9461d03-b412-574d-9841-bce08dceb69a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450531} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a1e573c-09ca-594f-a9ba-3b2660311b38" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d83cac8f-f51f-527a-b720-44ad32045870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450532} {:answer/id #uuid "333f0aac-7943-517c-87c6-fb5685a1c0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450533}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d83cac8f-f51f-527a-b720-44ad32045870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450532} :answer-field/id #uuid "611505c9-97d8-564c-8e0d-e557fa6b701e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451220} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e9805a-d060-5fce-8210-0537d29aaf51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450534} {:answer/id #uuid "7605eb1c-ab43-5593-8185-dd779575eaf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7605eb1c-ab43-5593-8185-dd779575eaf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450535} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1ba82cd-be54-5931-a8f3-844d7c5db9bc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ea143c38-1de5-5608-b272-c1150223e597" :question/math-academy-id "q-341232" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $\\pi /2$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Using the periodicity of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $f$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$-periodic, any shift by a multiple of its period returns the same value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,f(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f((x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides equal $f(x),$ the statement is true for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166962 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "544f5154-5213-5898-a45d-4546bc8004c8" :knowledge-point/title "Understanding the Periodic Extension of a Function" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049306 :topic/id #uuid "eb34afb7-9644-525e-b42b-cf82816ee5e0" :topic/math-academy-id 3195 :topic/title "Introduction to Fourier Series"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e69687f-8c03-5c9a-8e81-2feebdb84cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450536} {:answer/id #uuid "c4734c66-b830-5012-9fb4-cfa4985ad9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4734c66-b830-5012-9fb4-cfa4985ad9fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450537} :answer-field/id #uuid "476d4817-0c5c-5c10-bc1d-2482d72d4c55" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451222} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d93f9e6-a8cb-54ef-ba01-0f9b1655833f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450538} {:answer/id #uuid "556f5d27-0aa0-5d57-a7b0-29269a93cd55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d93f9e6-a8cb-54ef-ba01-0f9b1655833f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450538} :answer-field/id #uuid "955c3f26-0647-5690-bdcf-926edd101436" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451223} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea24cc05-96b2-58c0-adce-93dd4750e88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450540} {:answer/id #uuid "7a41e6fe-ab8d-5f68-b3fc-c4be05e1b48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a41e6fe-ab8d-5f68-b3fc-c4be05e1b48e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450541} :answer-field/id #uuid "92cf34f6-8dc7-5f8c-a110-8a694be94a2f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "47637532-5815-59ac-9760-d5eb87953b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341237" :question/problem "Let $F(x)=cos\\,(\\frac{x}{2})$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x+4\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **II.** $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n- **III.** $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\nf(x+4\\pi )=f((x+2\\pi )+2\\pi )=f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=2\\pi .$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2\\pi ) & =f(0+2\\pi )=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\nF(2\\pi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity: \n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c45b9636-399b-57c9-b0ca-5b056cab6e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467154} {:answer/id #uuid "00f0df38-33bd-5952-938b-70e46d37d368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c45b9636-399b-57c9-b0ca-5b056cab6e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467154} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc7f8e8-fc48-5fd1-b18c-379caef6afe3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468298} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4176da4a-c07c-53da-b9f8-81f889069292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467156} {:answer/id #uuid "37103a6e-807c-5169-b7ba-686822f478af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4176da4a-c07c-53da-b9f8-81f889069292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467156} :answer-field/id #uuid "3627c95d-cad1-56d5-b0b6-1badb632532d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b796237-8562-520b-9624-44bf611de202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467158} {:answer/id #uuid "58db0137-2f48-5c3e-a9ce-c6b13667728c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58db0137-2f48-5c3e-a9ce-c6b13667728c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467159} :answer-field/id #uuid "c76ce559-9343-5371-bc06-64f8880bfebd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "278579d2-6c97-5a07-b8af-1fbac0d66174" :question/math-academy-id "q-34174" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4bd3b326c89316bc916658421bcd4c65c32a72672af9fbd7aa5397cb53ab374.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  lies inside the given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a subspace. It represents the whole plane, which is considered a special subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/id #uuid "0cc17781-65fe-5316-8a26-4416142da248" :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea42219b-68d4-553e-8a3b-98282afd068a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218