7592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467164} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6330dd0-bf95-589a-81e7-1c0c7e80b327" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8ba2fd2-65b4-550d-8354-517b0631809b" :question/math-academy-id "q-342275" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n- **I.** $x=-4$\n- **II.** $x=-1$\n- **III.** $x=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/id #uuid "0cc17781-65fe-5316-8a26-4416142da248" :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "565302b3-a694-5119-832c-02e4fd6804c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467166} {:answer/id #uuid "d80b2c98-59a6-572c-99a4-544bc607fbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d80b2c98-59a6-572c-99a4-544bc607fbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467167} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ff76d8-87ef-508f-a045-32889e4d594a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468304} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddf69dfa-c941-5dc0-a9fe-85625a39ebc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467168} {:answer/id #uuid "fb67eda2-20bc-5279-b051-44e3d86c7e32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf69dfa-c941-5dc0-a9fe-85625a39ebc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9c19019-5f2d-5f70-a660-b1e6eafaae99" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468305} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4348e442-f651-55fa-8810-f010a007b915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467170} {:answer/id #uuid "8c269e3a-d8c4-506e-a5d9-0ddc16e4ff9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4348e442-f651-55fa-8810-f010a007b915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467170} :answer-field/id #uuid "d422223f-ce6a-5549-a13c-f416915547c3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "905b994c-71d1-59e7-beab-bb9f96b34458" :question/math-academy-id "q-342279" :question/problem "$$\n(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=3$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-3){y}^{′}+2xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-3}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{2x}{x+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-3}{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{2x}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-1,$ since both denominators at $x=-1$ equal zero. So, $x=-1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=3.$ So, $x=3$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317231 :knowledg