8308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b403b03-7155-5bb5-aa8c-bc5ff33fab4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467176} {:answer/id #uuid "8102d91e-7b5e-5d11-92cb-79ad6e8f099d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b403b03-7155-5bb5-aa8c-bc5ff33fab4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467176} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d078cd6-8e76-5adb-8a12-87518ea055da" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aae5f1f1-0d61-5bac-bdd4-df8a71221a89" :question/math-academy-id "q-342290" :question/problem "$$\n(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n- **I.** $x=-3$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=2$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Ordinary and Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/id #uuid "0cc17781-65fe-5316-8a26-4416142da248" :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "428cde04-ab5e-58de-b992-86b7cf2e9530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467178} {:answer/id #uuid "864cc451-97e9-5d2d-8285-7c47a1d19f4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428cde04-ab5e-58de-b992-86b7cf2e9530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467178} :answer-field/id #uuid "522b535d-a8d0-5112-8992-cd3d9c422181" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4092a0e7-4fdb-5777-ac5a-1d6461a5681e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467180} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c60f69-4e00-5fcb-b915-03fd86972b65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id 