d form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\frac{1}{x+2}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ while the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/id #uuid "0cc17781-65fe-5316-8a26-4416142da248" :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "718e262f-702d-56a3-8244-93f04bfef53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467214} {:answer/id #uuid "d33c017a-0f5d-5aa8-ba9c-c5d244f3eff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "718e262f-702d-56a3-8244-93f04bfef53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id 