/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467224} {:answer/id #uuid "640f8725-f57b-500a-a639-2a062c60e52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "640f8725-f57b-500a-a639-2a062c60e52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467225} :answer-field/id #uuid "113903cb-1bc5-536f-9fd3-01031005c466" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6886daeb-f858-5dc5-8034-d2a1fce18371" :question/math-academy-id "q-342369" :question/problem "$$\n(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x-4)P(x)$ and $(x-4)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=4.$\n- **III.** The point $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{x-4}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=4,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=4.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{2}{(x-4)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x-4}\n$$\n\nis also *not* analytic about $x=4$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=4.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=4$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=4$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Regular and Irregular Singular Points" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049703 :topic/id #uuid "0cc17781-65fe-5316-8a26-4416142da248" :topic/math-academy-id 1880 :topic/title "Regular Singular Points"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ebf5998-7225-5e38-81dc-9e7b52903e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467226} {:answer/id #uuid "19d931fc-b473-5940-8945-32b0c24cefeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ebf5998-7225-5e38-81dc-9e7b52903e77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467226} :answer-field/id #uuid "c25b5abe-6c02-5e7a-932d-99aad6b600d0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468334} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc8d16e9-f6ea-5032-a91e-3c9c5affa5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467228} {:answer/id #uuid "7a424ba5-0cc9-5de3-9fab-956b49e4fd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a424ba5-0cc9-5de3-9fab-956b49e4fd6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467229} :answer-field/id #uuid "206010b4-b1d1-5ab8-a173-e7115dc552b4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468335} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f926fbdf-68d1-56e8-baa8-7beed5b3a2e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467230} {:answer/id #uuid "20532180-bff8-5ab0-935b-eea7521a5570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20532180-bff8-5ab0-935b-eea7521a5570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467231} :answer-field/id #uuid "835e8348-52c5-5423-8672-4671ca915c37" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd3443d8-893d-55ee-8ecb-d75beee76ce3" :question/math-academy-id "q-342479" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- **II.** The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- **III.** The point $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x)=(x-5)\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{2x}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=5,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}Q(x)=(x-5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{(x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426 :topic/id #uuid "47f4b767-dd8b-5a07-a614-8ff85185d0eb" :topic/math-academy-id 1861 :topic/title "Linear Dependence and Independence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bd56f36-065b-5f6f-ada7-59d0d4edc3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467232} {:answer/id #uuid "c501bb1b-5855-5fe5-aa04-343c279dda15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bd56f36-065b-5f6f-ada7-59d0d4edc3a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467232} :answer-field/id #uuid "830bb6e9-6304-572e-9484-2e12a1e448a7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f385f2-bde1-507e-954a-ee9698ad5e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467234} {:answer/id #uuid "734b6d3c-c844-5e3c-a3c1-19569585738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4f385f2-bde1-507e-954a-ee9698ad5e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467234} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7540c27-cbb9-5003-ac7f-71412b58324b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3258c60-8ad9-5c0a-afbb-32fc33dd479d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467236} {:answer/id #uuid "8a77eb39-f7c2-5f5a-8e60-dade2d4ce331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a77eb39-f7c2-5f5a-8e60-dade2d4ce331" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467237} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3187b9c-cb03-5104-b346-f1118d08812b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1120ac93-1146-5212-b323-41cae9ac4014" :question/math-academy-id "q-34276" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\nII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+5{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26487427-5f9b-54b2-bf84-c50ca7d67c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467238} {:answer/id #uuid "8abe74c0-b916-592e-8e38-169447a7be01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26487427-5f9b-54b2-bf84-c50ca7d67c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467238} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb369ea-eb84-5999-9ef8-b0df4c5228f7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f749e06-8ca2-52aa-9e98-1b67c0b6a908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467240} {:answer/id #uuid "c92f06ac-0ec3-5893-aea1-dd43200715b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c92f06ac-0ec3-5893-aea1-dd43200715b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467241} :answer-field/id #uuid "dffd8e82-1715-566d-a74e-b5ca9f8ee2b6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21aeab67-d310-5a4e-9331-241f8caf4bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467242} {:answer/id #uuid "6101f1aa-f296-59f7-8d09-2eac34095f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21aeab67-d310-5a4e-9331-241f8caf4bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467242} :answer-field/id #uuid "56cc4e03-77d2-5272-af50-419f510cef6e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "113a385d-0512-508f-a50c-e45bc26c3c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34300" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"} :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434588} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434597}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077 :topic/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a32be64-f2b5-5cb3-a9b9-ef2943408008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467244} {:answer/id #uuid "07c1dfb9-72a8-504a-b11e-8c01835fe7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c1dfb9-72a8-504a-b11e-8c01835fe7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467245} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a85601-cde0-5741-a206-f9b2c5d853b4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468343} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aff10804-82e4-5d83-a8ad-c843c8e8a0a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467246} {:answer/id #uuid "5842b0a4-3f5b-582b-8bee-6e80b74c5259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5842b0a4-3f5b-582b-8bee-6e80b74c5259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467247} :answer-field/id #uuid "192b3bcd-ac72-5cdf-9221-db424cc98b06" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d159a5b-2686-5bfa-b54b-4ca0ed609fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467248} {:answer/id #uuid "2528c821-b847-54f0-8883-6d9ed55b4f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2528c821-b847-54f0-8883-6d9ed55b4f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467249} :answer-field/id #uuid "918b7c5a-dd99-5cbf-b43f-6939bcb541bb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92efc5e4-6fcf-5867-b774-9cceef4cda9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34327" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x=2y\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:x-y=0\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It actually represents the whole space ${R}^{2}$, since the coordinates of any vector $⟨x,y⟩$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x=2y$ since $0=2\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is a subspace. It also represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $x-y=0$ since $0-0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $C.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91737558-6c05-5704-a1bd-97256ba8ac71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467250} {:answer/id #uuid "d54efb51-dae9-51e7-b753-b49c7cc205e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d54efb51-dae9-51e7-b753-b49c7cc205e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467251} :answer-field/id #uuid "3550657e-00e5-5321-84f3-a02f359e43d1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468346} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e027dfef-3f17-5a98-aa1a-cd6ebb8d1099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467252} {:answer/id #uuid "3c812a6a-0c0e-5d30-a276-7f0ed6bd7075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c812a6a-0c0e-5d30-a276-7f0ed6bd7075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467253} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4abc7d-91c0-59ab-bd90-0b73bea7fd8e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468347} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2ae926-4da0-5141-8140-53b3f5f56040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467254} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2a20f6-f926-52e2-8658-01e2e788fd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2ae926-4da0-5141-8140-53b3f5f56040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467254} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cf5bb9c-c605-5d0f-b975-f7f042212076" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c611f45-045c-5502-9311-b6daf9203ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-343739" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Forced Harmonic Motion Equations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d69105-ab2d-55c3-9612-ee987e634694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08119a57-31c4-59b9-8f0b-94a273b4bb8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467256} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d8b9ffd-965a-5c5b-8676-57da40fbb9a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468349} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31720ab0-69d4-52f5-b3cb-fcd1e2ec0d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467258} {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1532792a-3167-5d17-8ad8-5abe35456b06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467259} :answer-field/id #uuid "46b86baf-0e5a-524c-b103-8dac5824fae3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} {:answer/id #uuid "6239f5bd-e810-5e80-93e2-7e27a749a934" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0585d6a-1830-5479-8da2-a0cc0d62a4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad5a3574-1760-5b93-87fd-b5f37a0870f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cf1f6c6-b17b-5c90-a172-f49c17abaa8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343746" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants.\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $4.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =4.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =4$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[16(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)]+16t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 16D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-16C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{8}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\frac{5}{8}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$\n\nOnly solution II matches the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"} :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441789} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441796} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048966 :topic/id #uuid "cfd9de67-b6d3-5aac-81ab-498c732e22a6" :topic/math-academy-id 6720 :topic/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems"} :activity/title "Resonance in Vibrating Systems" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482797} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483466}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317430 :knowledge-point